---
/**
 * Where the traditional material on this site comes from.
 *
 * Every reference page separates "traditional view" from "modern view" from
 * "Mo's synthesis" — a genuinely rigorous structure that was, until this page
 * existed, unattributed. Naming a doctrine's source is the difference between
 * "astrologers have always said" and a claim a reader can go and check, which is
 * the whole basis of the authority this site asks for.
 *
 * Every text listed here is a real, published, findable work. Nothing is
 * paraphrased into existence, and nothing claims Mo has personally studied a
 * given edition — the page describes the canon the material follows, which is
 * what is actually true.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import AuthorNote from '../../components/AuthorNote.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Sources', href: '/explore/sources/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sources — where the traditional material comes from"
  description="The classical and modern texts behind the traditional astrology on this site: Ptolemy, Dorotheus, Valens, Bonatti, Lilly, and the scholars who recovered them. Named so you can check them."
  path="/explore/sources/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Where this comes from</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Every reference page here separates the traditional view from the modern one and from my
          own synthesis. This page names what the traditional column actually rests on, so you can
          go and read it yourself rather than take my word for it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.src}
        srcset={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.alt}
        width={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.width}
        height={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.height}
        style="transform: scaleX(-1)"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why I bother naming them</h2>
      <p>
        Astrology has a bad habit of saying "the ancients believed" and leaving it there. It sounds
        authoritative and it can't be checked, which is the opposite of authority. When a page here
        says <em>traditional view</em>, it means a specific body of technique written down by
        specific people, most of whom you can read in English translation.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'll also say plainly what this page is not: a claim that older is truer. The traditional
        material and the modern material disagree in places, and where they do, the reference pages
        say so rather than quietly picking a winner.
      </p>

      <h2>The Hellenistic foundation</h2>
      <p>
        Nearly everything recognisable as Western astrology — houses, aspects, dignities, sect —
        was assembled in the Greek-speaking Mediterranean between roughly the first century BCE and
        the fourth century CE.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Dorotheus of Sidon, <em>Carmen Astrologicum</em></strong> (1st century CE) — the
          earliest substantially surviving systematic handbook. The source of much of what is still
          taught about triplicity rulers and timing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Claudius Ptolemy, <em>Tetrabiblos</em></strong> (2nd century CE) — the most
          influential single text in the tradition. The dignity tables this site calls "Ptolemaic"
          are his, and they remained the backbone of Western practice for well over a millennium.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vettius Valens, <em>Anthology</em></strong> (2nd century CE) — a working
          astrologer's casebook rather than a theory text, and the reason we know what Hellenistic
          practice actually looked like in use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Firmicus Maternus, <em>Mathesis</em></strong> (4th century CE) — the major Latin
          compendium, which carried the Hellenistic material westward.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Persian and medieval transmission</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Abu Ma'shar</strong> (9th century) — central to the transmission of Hellenistic
          astrology into Arabic scholarship, and from there back into Latin Europe.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guido Bonatti, <em>Liber Astronomiae</em></strong> (13th century) — the great
          medieval synthesis, and a standard reference for horary and electional technique.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The English horary tradition</h2>
      <p>
        This is the strand I'm formally trained in, so I'll be specific about it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>William Lilly, <em>Christian Astrology</em></strong> (1647) — the foundational
          English-language text on horary, and still the reference practitioners argue with. My
          Practitioner's Certificate in Horary Astrology is from the
          <a href="https://www.sta.co/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">School of Traditional Astrology</a>,
          which teaches within this lineage.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the old material became available again</h2>
      <p>
        Most of these texts were unavailable in usable English translation until surprisingly
        recently. If you have wondered why "traditional astrology" seems to have appeared out of
        nowhere in the last few decades, this is why — it is a recovery, not an invention.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Project Hindsight</strong> (from 1993) — the translation project, associated with
          Robert Hand, Robert Schmidt and Robert Zoller, that put Hellenistic and medieval sources
          into modern astrologers' hands.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Chris Brennan, <em>Hellenistic Astrology: The Study of Fate and Fortune</em></strong>
          (2017) — the standard modern scholarly overview of the Hellenistic system.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deborah Houlding</strong> — modern scholarship on house meanings and horary
          method, widely used in the traditional revival.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.keys} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Where modern practice comes from instead</h2>
      <p>
        When a page here says <em>modern view</em>, it generally means the psychological turn
        astrology took in the twentieth century — Alan Leo's reframing of the chart as character
        rather than fate, and later Dane Rudhyar's humanistic astrology, which recast the chart as a
        map of becoming rather than a fixed verdict.
      </p>
      <p>
        The three outer planets belong to this era for the obvious reason that nobody could see them
        before it: Uranus was found in 1781, Neptune in 1846, Pluto in 1930. Any statement about
        what they "rule" is necessarily modern, and the reference pages label it that way.
      </p>

      <h2>And when it's just me</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Mo's synthesis</em> means exactly that — my own reading, formed from the above and from
        the charts I've actually sat with. It's labelled separately because you deserve to know when
        you're reading a tradition and when you're reading one astrologer's opinion.
      </p>

      <AuthorNote />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Prefer to see it working?"
    body="A reading is where all of this stops being reference material and starts being about your life."
  />
</BaseLayout>
